+#### Runtime-control acceptance chain
+
+When a launcher, automation bridge, test harness, remote-control protocol, or
+similar tool is used to exercise the delivered runtime:
+
+1. Treat controller availability, a successful launch response, a process ID, and
+   an advertised control port as fixture preparation only. Require the target
+   process to remain alive and complete the controller handshake before crediting a
+   live connection.
+2. Record the controller and launcher versions, target application/runtime version,
+   project-pinned version, instance path, and candidate artifact hash and install
+   location. A mismatched target version is auxiliary compatibility evidence only,
+   never exact-pin acceptance.
+3. Prove the candidate is the code under test through the runtime's authoritative
+   mod/plugin/component list or logs. Then prove entry into the relevant title,
+   world, server, or workspace; query live state through the controller; exercise
+   the required behavior; and verify rollback or disablement where applicable.
+4. If an installer reports failure but leaves partial files, registrations, or a
+   launchable entry, the installation remains failed. Inspect the earliest fixture
+   construction error and do not credit leftovers or retry the same operation
+   blindly.
+5. Distinguish a third-party fixture construction or handshake failure from a
+   product failure. Do not infer a product result when the candidate was absent,
+   never loaded, or the control connection was never established.
+
+#### Cross-platform dependency closure
+
+When a project declares more than one supported operating system or architecture:
+
+1. Exercise dependency locking and verification on every declared target before
+   naming a build or release candidate. A lock or verification report produced on
+   one host is not evidence for native libraries, classifiers, POMs, or module
+   metadata selected only on another target.
+2. Record exact versions and verify platform-selected artifacts with published
+   checksums. When two authoritative repositories expose the same artifact, compare
+   their checksums before admitting newly discovered native or classifier files.
+3. Do not weaken dependency locking or verification globally to accommodate an
+   operating-system-specific resolution difference. Any exception must be narrow,
+   documented, and retain exact-version constraints plus artifact verification for
+   the excluded dependency.
+4. Treat every declared target as a required clean-runner gate. A matrix entry that
+   was not executed is unverified, even when another target passed an equivalent
+   build and test command.
+
+#### Human approval-gated milestone closure
+
+When a project contract requires explicit human acceptance or sign-off:
+
+1. Green executable evidence establishes candidate readiness, not milestone
+   acceptance. Do not mark the milestone complete, advance to the next phase, create
+   a release, or claim human approval from tests alone.
+2. Never infer approval from commit or push authorization, review activity, silence,
+   prior approval of another phase, or permission to continue routine implementation.
+   Await an explicit decision scoped to the named candidate or milestone.
+3. Once a decision arrives, preserve the exact human statement, decision date,
+   accepted immutable revision, accepted limitations, and the boundaries of actions
+   that remain separately authorized.
+4. Keep phase accounting exact: acceptance of one phase does not make higher-phase
+   gates pass. Higher-phase gates remain `NOT RUN` until their own evidence exists.
+5. Record acceptance in the project evidence and verify the acceptance-record commit
+   without rewriting the previously accepted candidate's measurements.
+
