 The ledger is evidence of evolution. A ledger entry, version bump, changelog edit, or formatting-only diff never counts as the meaningful improvement by itself.
 
+## 2026-10-11 — Runtime-control acceptance chain
+
+**Observed issue:** a runtime-control service accepted an install and launch request,
+registered a partially installed target, and returned a process ID plus advertised
+control port. The process then died before the control handshake, the installed
+loader did not match the project's pin, and the candidate product was never
+installed. Without an explicit evidence chain, these preparation signals could be
+misreported as runtime execution or as a product failure.
+
+**Meaningful improvement:** enforce mode now separates controller availability,
+launch acknowledgement, process liveness, handshake, candidate installation and
+load proof, application/world entry, live state queries, behavior execution, and
+rollback into distinct acceptance steps. It also keeps partial installer leftovers
+failed, restricts mismatched-version results to auxiliary evidence, and requires
+third-party fixture failures to be separated from product failures.
+
+**Future failure reduced:** agents are less likely to accept a returned PID as a
+running controlled application, test the wrong loader version without disclosure,
+credit an artifact that was never loaded, repeatedly invoke a broken installer, or
+blame the product for a fixture that failed before product execution.
+
+**Changed paths:** `SKILL.md`, `tools/validate-source.py`, `VERSION`, `SOURCE.json`,
+`CHANGELOG.md`, and `evolution/LEDGER.md`.
+
+**Validation target:** `python tools/validate-source.py`, canonical source diff
+checks, and clean application of the cumulative patch to its frozen base. Canonical
+CI and the evolution guard remain required after any future authorized publication.
+
+## 2026-10-11 — Human approval-gated milestone closure
+
+**Observed issue:** a project completed every executable phase gate and produced a
+green cross-platform candidate, but its binding acceptance contract still required
+an explicit human decision. Commit and push authorization had already been granted,
+creating a risk that routine repository authority could be mistaken for milestone
+acceptance or that the next phase could start before the actual decision.
+
+**Meaningful improvement:** enforce mode now distinguishes candidate readiness from
+human acceptance, forbids inferring approval from adjacent permissions or silence,
+and requires the exact decision, date, immutable accepted revision, limitations, and
+remaining authorization boundaries to be recorded. It also requires higher-phase
+gates to remain `NOT RUN` until independently evidenced.
+
+**Future failure reduced:** agents are less likely to self-approve a human-gated
+milestone, advance phases from green CI alone, conflate source push permission with
+release authority, or upgrade unevaluated future gates when recording acceptance.
+
+**Changed paths:** `SKILL.md`, `tools/validate-source.py`, `VERSION`, `SOURCE.json`,
+`CHANGELOG.md`, and `evolution/LEDGER.md`.
+
+**Validation target:** `python tools/validate-source.py`, canonical source diff
+checks, and clean application of the cumulative patch to its frozen base. Canonical
+CI and the evolution guard remain required after any future authorized publication.
+
+## 2026-10-11 — Cross-platform dependency closure before candidate status
+
+**Observed issue:** dependency locking and verification passed on a Windows host,
+but a clean Linux runner selected additional native transport and LWJGL classifier
+artifacts that were absent from the Windows-generated lock and verification state.
+A single-host report therefore overstated closure for a declared multi-platform
+build.
+
+**Meaningful improvement:** enforce mode now requires dependency locking and
+verification on every declared operating-system and architecture target before
+candidate status. It explicitly covers target-selected natives, classifiers, POMs,
+and module metadata; requires authoritative checksum corroboration when available;
+and permits only narrow documented exceptions that preserve exact-version and
+artifact-integrity checks.
+
+**Future failure reduced:** agents are less likely to certify dependency closure
+from one development host, disable verification broadly when a clean runner finds
+an OS-specific artifact, or discover missing native checksums only after delivery.
+
+**Changed paths:** `SKILL.md`, `tools/validate-source.py`, `VERSION`, `SOURCE.json`,
+`CHANGELOG.md`, and `evolution/LEDGER.md`.
+
+**Validation target:** `python tools/validate-source.py`, canonical source diff
+checks, and a clean application of the cumulative patch followed by the same
+validator. Canonical CI and the evolution guard remain required after any future
+authorized publication.
+
+## 2026-10-09 — Truth-preserving fallback for unavailable host intake
+
+**Observed issue:** a host required a named read-only workspace intake provider,
+but the provider was absent from the tool catalog and its MCP server was
+unregistered. Without an explicit fallback rule, an agent could falsely imply the
+intake ran, stop despite sufficient local routing law, or continue without making
+the degraded condition visible.
+
+**Meaningful improvement:** the canonical skill now requires an attempted named
+intake before substantive project actions, exact disclosure when it is unavailable,
+and a bounded fallback to host-named local entry documents only when they establish
+the project boundary, authority constraints, and safe routing. A host-declared hard
+stop still blocks work.
+
+**Future failure reduced:** governed work is less likely to proceed on fabricated
+intake evidence or to deadlock unnecessarily when an optional connector is missing,
+while host authority and fail-closed behavior remain intact.
+
+**Changed paths:** `SKILL.md`, `tools/validate-source.py`, `VERSION`, `SOURCE.json`,
+`CHANGELOG.md`, and `evolution/LEDGER.md`.
+
+**Validation target:** `python tools/validate-source.py`, a missing-provider review
+case, canonical CI, and the evolution guard after an authorized commit.
+
+## 2026-10-09 — Version-tolerant resolver start-path invocation
+
+**Observed issue:** an installed generated mirror accepted `-StartPath`, while an
+invocation guessed `-WorkspacePath` and failed at PowerShell parameter binding before
+pack discovery could run. Different accepted mirror revisions can otherwise turn a
+valid local pack into a false “not found” diagnosis.
+
+**Meaningful improvement:** the canonical resolver now accepts `-WorkspacePath` as
+an alias for `-StartPath`; Step 0 requires parameter discovery against the installed
+resolver before passing a start path; and source validation protects the alias from
+regression.
+
+**Future failure reduced:** agents are less likely to treat a mirror-schema mismatch
+as a missing Constitution pack, skip binding law, or waste time retrying guessed
+parameters.
+
+**Changed paths:** `SKILL.md`, `scripts/resolve-pack.ps1`,
+`tools/validate-source.py`, `VERSION`, `SOURCE.json`, `CHANGELOG.md`,
+`evolution/LEDGER.md`.
+
+**Validation target:** `python tools/validate-source.py`, PowerShell parser checks,
+both `-StartPath` and `-WorkspacePath` resolver invocations against the same valid
+pack, and canonical CI/evolution guard after an authorized commit.
+
